{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PLACEMENT DECISION for verify-completion (the open question this ticket was filed to resolve).\n\nThe parent (eclectic-industrial-argali) explicitly left it open: 'verify-completion has no coverage object at all, so it needs a placement decision first'. Nothing in either ticket's recorded context decides it, so this is a judgement call and is recorded here rather than only in code.\n\nDecision: coverage.llm_step_failures, mirroring review-plan and review-code, with the 'coverage' object created ONLY when the tally is non-empty.\n\nWhy:\n1. Consistency across gates. One JSON path works for all three, so a caller reads the tally the same way everywhere. A bespoke top-level key on this one gate would make the tally gate-specific for no gain, and the sink's shape ({total, by_step}) is already identical.\n2. Attestation safety. Creating 'coverage' only when non-empty keeps a clean run's verdict byte-identical -- no empty container on the overwhelmingly common path. That matters more here than on the other two gates because the completion verdict is SIGNED, and byte-identity on clean runs is the posture the parent established.\n\nRejected alternative: a top-level 'llm_step_failures' key on completion_verdict. It avoids introducing a one-key 'coverage' object, but breaks the cross-gate path and buys nothing, since the object is absent entirely on clean runs anyway.\n\nThe completion_verdict schema is additionalProperties:true, so declaring 'coverage' documents the key rather than unblocking it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786561880145641001,"uuid":"95796dce-7faf-4f8c-a46b-5962f616069d"}